               <forename>Nicolò</forename> <surname>Vidacovich</surname>
            </persName>
            <persName type="full">Nicolò Vidacovich</persName>
            <date type="birth" when="1875-11-18"/>
            <date type="death" when="1934-06-17"/>
            <note type="gender">M</note>
         </ab>
         <div type="bio">
            <p>Triestine lawyer. Vidacovich took English lessons from JJ in Trieste, and was involved in JJ’s establishment of the Volta Cinematograph in Dublin in 1909. The two collaborated in translating into Italian <name type="person" key="ID_MISSING">Synge’s</name> <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Riders to the Sea</name> (published in 1929 as “La cavalcata al mare”, <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Solaria</name> 4, no. 9-10 [September-October 1929], 3-16) and at least part of Yeats’s <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">The Countess Cathleen</name>. <hi rend="bold">William Brockman</hi>
                </p>
         </div>
